course: dictionary
lesson: 9663
title: Substation: a place, not a station
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A substation is a place, often fenced in, full of large electrical equipment. Its job is to change electricity so it can be sent safely to homes, schools and shops.
explain: «Power lines and substations are for engineers only.» The clue is in what the sentence says about wires and fences. You can also check the word: a substation «lowers the voltage», which means it reduces how strong the electrical push is before the power reaches our streets and houses.
explain: The word can also mean a small branch office, like a police substation. Ask yourself which meaning fits: is the sentence about electricity, fences and wires, or about a building where police work? The rest of the sentence gives you the answer.
explain: Safety comes first. «Never climb a pylon or into an electricity substation, even for a ball.» Signs say «Danger of death». If a ball lands near one, do not go in. Tell an adult or call the power company.
fact: A substation is a place, often fenced in, with large electrical equipment.
fact: A substation changes the voltage of electricity so it can be sent to homes, schools and shops.
fact: Electricity travels from power stations along thick cables on tall pylons at very high voltage.
fact: A substation can also be a small branch office, like a police substation.
fact: Never climb into a substation or fetch a ball from one, because the electricity can kill. Tell an adult or call the power company.
exercise: In the lesson, what is a substation mainly for? | Making the wind blow | Carrying passengers across town | *Changing electricity so it can be sent to homes and shops | Storing water for the school
exercise: «Never climb a pylon or into an electricity substation, even for a ball.» What should you do if a ball lands inside a substation fence? | *Tell an adult or call the power company | Climb in quickly and fetch it | Wait until it gets dark | Throw a stone at it to knock it out
exercise: Ella reads, «She went to the police substation to report her lost bike.» What does substation mean here? | *A small branch office | A powerful electrical push | A fenced place full of electrical equipment | A kind of pylon
test: In «a substation lowers the voltage before it reaches our streets», what does lowers suggest? | Makes the electricity louder | *Reduces how strong the electrical push is | Turns the electricity off for ever | Paints the cables green
test: Why is a substation usually fenced in? | *To keep people away from dangerous electrical equipment | To keep the grass tidy | To stop the wind from blowing | To keep animals in the field
exam: Which sentence shows the correct safety action near a substation? | Poke a stick through the fence to reach the ball | *Keep away, and tell an adult if a ball lands inside | Climb the fence to see inside | Touch the wires with wet hands
